# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-08-superposition/blob/main/pkg/seawater/src/wasm_erc20.rs#L90
https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-08-superposition/blob/main/pkg/seawater/src/wasm_erc20.rs#L100


# Vulnerability details

## Impact

In the current implementation of `wasm_erc20` module, functions for encoding and calling ERC20 functions are provided. The problem is that when encoding `transfer()` and `transferFrom()`, address type is assumed to have 32 bytes when in reality it has 20 bytes. This can lead to consistently paying more gas when sending transactions as there will be more bytes allocated than needed.

## Proof of Concept

`transfer()` and `transferFrom()` functions are encoded the following way:

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-08-superposition/blob/main/pkg/seawater/src/wasm_erc20.rs#L100-108
```
fn encode_transfer_from(from: Address, to: Address, amount: U256) -> [u8; 4 + 32 + 32 + 32] {
    let mut data = [0_u8; 4 + 32 * 3];
    write_selector(&mut data, &TRANSFER_FROM_SELECTOR);
    write_address(&mut data, 0, from);
    write_address(&mut data, 1, to);
    write_u256(&mut data, 2, amount);

    data
}

```

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-08-superposition/blob/main/pkg/seawater/src/wasm_erc20.rs#L90-97
```
/// Encodes a call to `transfer(address to, uint256 amount)`
fn encode_transfer(to: Address, amount: U256) -> [u8; 4 + 32 + 32] {
    let mut data = [0_u8; 4 + 32 * 2];
    write_selector(&mut data, &TRANSFER_SELECTOR);
    write_address(&mut data, 0, to);
    write_u256(&mut data, 1, amount);

    data
}
```

Data is calculated to be 68 bytes for `transfer()` when in reality address type just takes 20 bytes meaning it should be `4 + 32 + 20 = 56 bytes`. The same situation applies to the `transfer_from()`. This can lead to a situation where the protocol consistently pays more gas as it sends more payload bytes than needed.

## Tools Used

Manual review.

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

Recalculate the number of bytes needed for the function parameters.


